// When people take up arms without being told to.
//
// Nobody recruits militia. A region raises it when there is an enemy on or near its ground,
// and how readily depends on whether it thinks the state is theirs. A region of the
// country's own people, that lives decently and still believes in the war, fills a
// formation in weeks; a region held down by a state it does not recognise fills none at
// all, however many people live in it. That asymmetry is the whole point - it is the same
// number the secession rules read, from the other side.
//
// Each day a threatened region adds to its readiness:
//
//     base
//   * (own_people_share ^ nationality)      who the people think the state is for
//   * (war_support_floor + war_support * national war support)
//   * (stability_floor + stability * regional stability)
//   * (threat_floor + threat * how close the enemy is)
//   * (population / population_per_point)
//   * whatever the government and the ideology are worth
//
// and when readiness passes 1 a formation stands up, paid for in manpower and nothing else.
{
  // A day's readiness for a region of `population_per_point` people with everything else
  // at one. The multipliers below only ever make it slower, and they compound: a front-line
  // oblast of a million people that is threatened but not occupied comes out around a
  // fiftieth a day, which is a formation in about two months. One actually overrun raises
  // one in three weeks.
  "base": 0.100,
  "population_per_point": 1000000,

  // How sharply the share of the country's own people matters. Above 1 it bites: a region
  // that is two thirds the state's own nationality raises less than two thirds as much.
  "nationality": 1.60,

  // Nobody rises for a war the country has given up on, but an invasion does not need
  // enthusiasm either - hence the floors.
  "war_support_floor": 0.25,
  "war_support": 0.75,

  "stability_floor": 0.45,
  "stability": 0.55,

  // How close the enemy has to be. Ground the enemy already holds counts fully; a region
  // with even one province facing them counts for the floor, because a border is not a
  // fraction - a region with an enemy army on one edge knows the enemy is there.
  "threat_floor": 0.35,
  "threat": 0.65,

  // The most militia formations one region will ever have standing at once, and the most a
  // country will. A country does not become a hundred militia divisions.
  "per_region": 3,
  "per_country": 40,

  // How many people one formation is, and how many a region needs before it can field one.
  // A village does not raise a division however frightened it is.
  //
  // It costs no manpower. That is the point: these are not reservists the ministry called
  // up, they are the people who already live there. Drawing them from the national reserve
  // produced exactly the opposite of the intent - a country that raised militia and then
  // could not replace its line divisions.
  "formation": 9000,
  "population_per_formation": 250000,

  // And how it goes home. Once no enemy has been near the region for this many days, the
  // formation dissolves - otherwise a war fought in 2026 leaves a country carrying forty
  // free divisions in 2040. The men go back to the region they were always in.
  "stand_down_days": 30
}
